# Why pull back a curtain? Vermeer's The Art of Painting
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "Who is the curtain pulled back for? Why paint the painter from behind?"
next: {"title":"Who is watching whom? Fragonard's The Swing","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/the-painting-knows-youre-there/07-who-is-watching-whom-fragonard-s-the.cast"}

## The Art of Painting: why pull back a curtain?
sources:
    - id: khm
      title: The Art of Painting (GG 9128)
      authors: Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna
      year: 2024
      finding: c. 1666/68; the model poses as Clio, muse of history; the map of the 17 provinces before their division refers to a land whose fame came from painting; the widow passed it to her mother in 1676
      url: https://khm.at/en/object/2574
    - id: wiki
      title: The Art of Painting
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Clio's laurel, trumpet and book; Visscher's map; the painter in a slashed doublet seen from behind; Vermeer never sold it; Catharina Bolnes transferred it to Maria Thins in 1676
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Art_of_Painting
    - id: fucci
      title: 'Parrhasius and the art of display: the illusionistic curtain in seventeenth-century Dutch painting'
      authors: Robert Fucci
      year: 2013
      finding: Curtains hung over Dutch paintings against dust and light; painters painted illusionistic curtains, Rembrandt's Kassel Holy Family (1646) the earliest dated
    - id: ev
      title: The Art of Painting
      authors: Essential Vermeer (Jonathan Janson)
      year: 2024
      finding: The tapestry is folded toward the viewer so that we enter the composition; the painter is applying paint to a laurel wreath whose leaves have turned blue
      url: https://www.essentialvermeer.com/cat_about/art.html

    card title "The Art of Painting"

Johannes Vermeer painted this studio in Delft in the late sixteen-sixties,
    image art cites ["khm"] credit "Johannes Vermeer, c. 1666–68. Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna. Public domain." x 318 y 330 width 555 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Jan_Vermeer_-_The_Art_of_Painting_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Jan_Vermeer_-_The_Art_of_Painting_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.book [0.404,0.474,0.073,0.074] regions.canvas [0.755,0.495,0.155,0.23] regions.chair [0.13,0.88,0.36,0.12] regions.chandelier [0.51,0.06,0.27,0.19] regions.clio [0.36,0.39,0.18,0.29] regions.clio_wreath [0.405,0.39,0.12,0.045] regions.curtain [0,0,0.44,1] regions.curtain_edge [0.27,0,0.18,0.6] regions.hitch [0.2,0.25,0.3,0.45] regions.map [0.38,0.18,0.57,0.32] regions.painted_wreath [0.765,0.505,0.072,0.035] regions.painter [0.565,0.42,0.32,0.52] regions.painter_head [0.66,0.42,0.125,0.095] regions.painter_view [0.38,0.4,0.62,0.5] regions.studio [0.36,0.06,0.64,0.88] regions.trumpet [0.266,0.483,0.166,0.045] regions.whole [0,0,1,1] regions.wreath_view [0.66,0.44,0.26,0.17]

and our question is: why pu (@highlight art:curtain@)ll back a curtain?

    camera on ["art:hitch"]

A heavy tapestry fills the left of the picture, hitched aside like a stage curtain, close enough to touch.
    highlight art:curtain strength strong lift true

    camera reset true

It hangs on our side, with a chair beside it;
    highlight art:chair strength strong lift true

the studio lies beyond.
    highlight art:studio lift true

In Dutch homes, real curtains often hung over costly paintings, to keep off dust and light.
    highlight art:curtain strength soft lift true

Painters made a game of that habit, and pa (@highlight art:curtain_edge strength strong lift true@)inted the curtain into the picture itself.

So who has this one been pulled back for?
    highlight art:studio strength soft lift true

    pause 0.4

Not for the model: she stands in the light, dressed as Clio, the muse of history.
    highlight art:clio strength strong lift true

Her laurel wreath stands for glory,
    highlight art:clio_wreath effect detail

the trumpet for fame,
    highlight art:trumpet effect detail

and the book for the history that keeps them.
    highlight art:book effect detail

    camera on ["art:painter_view"]

Not for the painter either: he sits with his back to us, absorbed, and never looks round.
    highlight art:painter strength strong lift true

    camera reset true

It is pulled back for us, into a room where nobody knows we are there.
    highlight art:curtain lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.58,0.4,0.38,0.34]"]

    ask "What is on his canvas so far? Tap it." spot painted_wreath on art
        intro: He is painting her. How far has he got?
        right: Just a wreath: a few leaves, and the rest still bare.
        wrong: Look past his shoulder, at the canvas on the easel.

    camera reset true

He began with the wreath, the sign of glory; once green, its leaves have turned blue over the centuries.
    highlight art:painted_wreath effect detail

Last time, in Las Meninas, Velázquez looked straight out at us from his canvas. Vermeer turns his painter round.
    highlight art:painter lift true

He is often taken for Vermeer himself, but with his face turned from us he could be any painter: less a portrait than the art of painting.
    highlight art:painter_head effect detail

He kept this picture: Vermeer never sold it, even in debt, and after his death his widow gave it to her mother to keep it from his creditors.
    highlight art:whole strength soft lift true

And from behind, we see wh (@highlight art:clio lift true@)at he sees: the model,

and the picture growing out of her.
    highlight art:canvas lift true

    camera on ["art:map"]

Behind her hangs a map of the seventeen provinces of the Netherlands, from before they split into north and south.
    highlight art:map lift true

The museum reads it as a land whose fame came from its painters.
    highlight art:map strength soft

    camera reset true

So the curtain is drawn back for us,
    highlight art:curtain rest grey lift true

and the painter turned away, so that we can watch without being watched.
    highlight art:painter strength strong rest grey lift true

    quiz "A film director wants viewers to feel they are secretly watching a pianist practise. Which shot follows Vermeer?"
        right: Through the door, from behind: she never looks round, and the doorway is our curtain.
        wrong: Vermeer gives us a way in, and nobody who looks back.
        * Her face, eyes on the lens
        * Over her shoulder, as she glances back
        + Through a half-open door, behind her
        * A full concert hall, from the stalls

Three and a half centuries on, the curtain is still open.
    highlight art:curtain lift true

Come in; he won't turn round.
    highlight art:painter lift true

